Abstraction is a process of hiding the implementation details and showing only functionality to the user.
There are two ways to achieve abstraction in Java:
  Using Abstract Class (0 to 100%)
  Using Interface (100%)

Why Abstraction needed?
| Problem          | How Abstraction Solves It            |
| ---------------- | ------------------------------------ |
| Complex logic    | Hides complexity                     |
| Tight coupling   | Promotes loose coupling              |
| Frequent changes | Implementation can change safely     |
| Hard testing     | Enables mocking                      |
| Poor design      | Encourages programming to interfaces |

4. Ways to Achieve Abstraction in Java
  4.1 Using Interface (100% Abstraction – conceptually)
      interface Payment {
          void pay(double amount);
      }
      class UpiPayment implements Payment {
          public void pay(double amount) {
              System.out.println("UPI payment: " + amount);
          }
      }

4.2 Using Abstract Class (Partial Abstraction)
  abstract class BankAccount {
      abstract double getInterestRate();
    
      public void deposit(double amount) {
          System.out.println("Deposited: " + amount);
      }
  }

  class SavingsAccount extends BankAccount {
      double getInterestRate() {
          return 4.5;
      }
  }

5. Interface vs Abstract Class (🔥 Must-Know)
| Feature              | Interface                 | Abstract Class      |
| -------------------- | ------------------------- | ------------------- |
| Abstraction          | Full (behavior)           | Partial             |
| Methods              | abstract, default, static | abstract + concrete |
| Variables            | public static final       | any                 |
| Constructors         | ❌ No                      | ✅ Yes               |
| Multiple inheritance | ✅ Yes                     | ❌ No                |
| State                | ❌ No                      | ✅ Yes               |
| Spring preference    | ✅ High                    | Medium              |

6. Abstraction vs Encapsulation (Very Common Question)
| Abstraction      | Encapsulation         |
| ---------------- | --------------------- |
| WHAT to expose   | HOW to protect        |
| Design level     | Implementation level  |
| Uses interfaces  | Uses access modifiers |
| Hides complexity | Hides data            |


7. Abstraction vs Polymorphism
| Abstraction             | Polymorphism      |
| ----------------------- | ----------------- |
| Defines contract        | Executes behavior |
| Design-time             | Runtime           |
| Uses interface/abstract | Uses overriding   |

8. Abstract Class – Important Rules
  Cannot be instantiated
  Can have:
    abstract methods
    concrete methods
    constructors
  Can have instance variables
  Can have access modifiers

abstract class Vehicle {
    abstract void start();

    Vehicle() {
        System.out.println("Vehicle created");
    }
}

9. Interface – Abstraction Rules (Java 8+)
interface Logger {

    void log(String msg); // abstract

    default void info(String msg) {
        log("INFO: " + msg);
    }

    static void printVersion() {
        System.out.println("v1.0");
    }
}

Default methods → backward compatibility
Static methods → utility behavior
Private methods (Java 9+) → internal reuse

10. Abstraction in Spring Boot (🔥 Very Important)
Service Layer Abstraction
public interface OrderService {
    void placeOrder();
}

@Service
public class OrderServiceImpl implements OrderService {
    public void placeOrder() {
        System.out.println("Order placed");
    }
}


👉 Controller depends on interface, not implementation.

Benefits in Spring
  Loose coupling
  Easy mocking (@MockBean)
  AOP support
  Easy replacement of implementation

11. Abstraction & Dependency Injection
@Autowired
private OrderService orderService;
  Spring injects concrete implementation
  Caller knows what, not how
  👉 This is abstraction + polymorphism in action.

15. Common Tricky Interview Questions (4+ Years)
Q1. Can abstraction exist without encapsulation?
❌ No (data must be protected)

Q2. Can abstract class have constructor?
✅ Yes (used for initialization)

Q3. Can interface have implementation?
✅ Yes (default & static methods)

Q4. Why use interface when abstract class exists?
Multiple inheritance
Cleaner contracts
Better Spring/AOP support

Q5. Is abstraction compile-time or runtime?
Design-time concept
Enforced at runtime via polymorphism

Q6. Can we create object of abstract class?
❌ No

